Hi Yuchao,

I have 3 question on Canopy which I am testing for my clonal evolution study:

Is Dirichlet process used to define optimal # of clones? If not, could it be implemented?
When running more then 100 variants (sna only mode) Canopy seems to have trouble to pass post step with cutoff = 0.05 and if I run longer (as suggested) it produces an error (subscript ouf of bounds, post not found etc). So is there a sweet spot on how many chains, burning, writeskip parameters to be used to produce meaningful results that would pass 0.05 cutoff?
How about sex chromosome variants. So if my SNA is on X chromosome and it is a male patient sample, should I specify it in CNA matrix since it would be like a "CNA" in a sense? SNA only mode only takes R and X and  Canopy is unaware of variants locations in this case.

Thank you

Best,

Chingiz

